====================================================================================================
 MarXus master equation
====================================================================================================
 RUN SETTINGS
 ------------
 Deck:               /home/peter/Dropbox/Research_Leuven/MarXus/validation/acetyl_o2_mesmer/input/acetyl_o2.inp
 Method:             CHEMICALLY SIGNIFICANT EIGENVALUES (CSE): phenomenological rate coefficients
                     (Miller, Klippenstein, JPCA 110, 10528 (2006); Georgievskii et al., JPCA 117, 12146 (2013), eqs. 21-30)
 Solvers:            all eigenpairs of the symmetrized J: FullDecompositionLapack; well-to-well matrix inverted by Gauss-Jordan elimination
                     species merging (Georgievskii et al. 2013, Sec. IV; partition as MESS): ChemicalEigenvalueMax 0.2 (deck), WellProjectionThreshold 0.2 (default); chemical eigenvectors: relaxational projection 1 - F_ne <= ChemicalEigenvalueMax (MESS direct method) (default)
 Parallel:           4 cores (--ncore); the 1 conditions (T, p) in batches of 1 at a time (rayon)
                     BLAS threads per LAPACK call: 4
 Temperatures (K):   298
 Pressures (torr):   200.72
 Energy grid:        grains of 100.000 cm-1 on a common absolute scale, sums of 1 cm-1 counting cells
 Collisions:         exponential down, exactly normalized (Robertson (ed.), CCK 43 (2019), eq. 4.16), transitions up to 15 <dE_down>
                     low-energy reservoirs where eq. 4.16 fails (sparse states, Robertson 2019, p. 294): the grains from the
                       failing one down form one thermalized state (reservoir state, MESMER manual, Sec. 14.2.1):
                       T = 298 K: Int1 1 grains (100 cm-1, 47.3 k_BT below the lowest threshold)
                     collision frequency: Lennard-Jones (per well, below); collision integral Omega(2,2)*: Neufeld, Janzen, Aziz, J. Chem. Phys. 57, 1100 (1972) (CollisionIntegral in the deck)
 Tunneling:          exact Eckart transmission (Miller, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 101, 6810 (1979), eq. 8)
 Source:             thermal reactant R through B_R1 (R -> Int1)
                     F(E) ~ rho(E) k(E) exp(-E/kT) of the entrance channels (Pfeifle, Olzmann, IJCK 46, 231 (2014), eqs. 7, 9)
====================================================================================================
 CHEMICAL NETWORK
 ----------------

Wells (collisions: <dE_down>(T) = <dE_down>(T_ref) (T/T_ref)^n; Lennard-Jones collision frequency):
      Name   grains    absolute grains   collisions; sink
      Int1      179         -117 .. 61   <dE_down> = 130 cm-1 (T/300 K)^0; sigma 3.820 A, epsilon 69.47 K, reduced mass 3.797 amu
      Int2      168         -106 .. 61   <dE_down> = 130 cm-1 (T/300 K)^0; sigma 3.820 A, epsilon 69.47 K, reduced mass 3.797 amu

Channels (k(E) of every channel; rate model from the deck):
      Name     From       To   Model
      B_R1     Int1        R   barrierless, inverse Laplace transform; entrance of the reactant (source)
      B_R2     Int1     Int2   rigid transition state, Eckart tunneling
      B_R4     Int1 P_ketene_HO2   rigid transition state
      B_R2     Int2     Int1   rigid transition state, Eckart tunneling
      B_R3     Int2 P_lactone_OH   rigid transition state

Energetics (kcal/mol, relative to R):

Wells (G - ground energy, D - lowest barrier out of the well):
      Name          G          D
      Int1     -33.34      -5.14
      Int2     -30.39      -8.49

Bimolecular (G - ground energy):
      Name          G
         R       0.00

Barriers (H - ZeroEnergy of the barrier in the deck):
      Name          H     From       To   Model
      B_R1       0.00     Int1        R   barrierless, inverse Laplace transform
      B_R2      -5.14     Int1     Int2   rigid transition state, Eckart tunneling
      B_R4       2.77     Int1 P_ketene_HO2   rigid transition state
      B_R3      -8.49     Int2 P_lactone_OH   rigid transition state

